      "findings": [
        {
          "severity": "Medium",
          "criterion": "paraphrase_fidelity",
          "location": "worked_examples[0].steps[1]",
          "problem": "The step says that at the retail emergency-kit device 'Schedule II stock must be loaded by a pharmacist, intern or certified technician'. That exclusive list omits the nurse. Policy 2019-02 Requirement 10 lets a Massachusetts-licensed nurse load such a device (10(b)) with no schedule limit. The only schedule limit (Schedules III through VI) is on the ordinary pharmacy technician in 10(a). The resolution ('works only if Schedule II loading is moved to an eligible loader') inherits the ambiguity.",
          "rationale": "The step contradicts the section's own comparison_tables[1].rows[0] ('Any, except that a pharmacy technician is limited to Schedules III through VI') and role_duties[3]. A candidate following the worked example would wrongly conclude that a nurse may not load Schedule II stock into a retail emergency-kit device. Suggested fix: 'Schedule II stock must be loaded by the nurse, a pharmacist, an intern or a certified technician, not by the ordinary technician.'",
          "authorities": [
            {
              "official_url": "https://www.mass.gov/doc/2019-02-automated-dispensing-device-use-pdf/download",
              "exact_section": "Requirement 10(a)-(b)",
              "checked_date": "2026-09-17"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "Low",
          "criterion": "material_exceptions",
          "location": "orientation[12].text; comparison_tables[3].rows[1].cells[1]",
          "problem": "The section states the child-resistant effectiveness figures as 85 percent without a demonstration and 80 percent after one. It omits the second sentence of 16 CFR 1700.15(b)(1): for unit packaging, child-resistant effectiveness is not less than 80 percent.",
          "rationale": "orientation[14] then applies 1700.15 to multi-drug-single-dose blister cards, which are unit packaging, so the unit-packaging figure is the relevant one. This is unlikely to be tested and does not change any dispensing decision, so it is Low.",
          "authorities": [
            {
              "official_url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-II/subchapter-E/part-1700",
              "exact_section": "16 CFR 1700.15(b)(1)",
              "checked_date": "2026-09-17"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "Low",
          "criterion": "material_exceptions",
          "location": "orientation[6].text",
          "problem": "In listing what Policy 2022-07 adds, the section omits the policy's preamble statement that, for a licensed healthcare facility, approval for use and placement of the APS must be obtained from the facility's licensing body (e.g., BHCSQ).",
          "rationale": "This is a narrow setting at the edge of the section's focus on retail pickup kiosks, and nothing the section says contradicts it. Adding it would make the policy account complete.",
          "authorities": [
            {
              "official_url": "https://www.mass.gov/doc/2022-07-automated-pharmacy-systems-pdf/download",
              "exact_section": "Preamble paragraph",
              "checked_date": "2026-09-17"
            }
          ]
        }
